{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments ["<b>Let us go up, and we shall possess it, for we are well able to overcome it.</b> The doubling of expressions may be explained in two ways. The first way is that 'עָלֹה' ('let [Her] go up') he said with reference to Above, in the manner of what is said (Genesis 46:4) 'וְאָנֹכִי אַעַלְךָ גַם עָלֹה' — which our Rabbis of blessed memory explained: 'גַם עָלֹה' refers to the Shekhinah (Mekhilta Beshallaḥ ch. 3), and along with Her, he too would go up. So too its explanation here: 'עָלֹה' — do you not concede to me regarding 'עָלֹה' that the Shekhinah will go up to the inn of Her dwelling, for that land is an inn for the Shekhinah from days of old, as our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Ḥullin 91b): 'This righteous man has come to the inn of his lodging.' And just as the Shekhinah will go up, so along with Her we too shall go up — we who cleave to HaShem. 'כִּי יָכוֹל' ('for [He] is able') — do you not concede to me regarding 'יָכוֹל' that the Holy One, blessed be He, is able against them, and just as He is able against them, so we too are able against them, through the power of El blessed be He who is with us. And the spies answered: from the very first premise the argument is flawed, and the initial assumption is incorrect — we cannot go up, for he is stronger than we are; as it were, they said this with reference to Above, and so Rashi explained." "The second way is that he said 'עָלֹה': it is true that the ascent is possible; but 'נַעֲלֶה' ('we shall go up') is on this condition — if we mend our deeds, for the matter depends on merit, and we are obliged to make preparation for the ascent through repentance and good deeds. And likewise 'יָכוֹל': the Holy One, blessed be He, is certainly able against them, but on this condition — that we too are able, by removing the heart of stone from our midst. And the spies answered: if the matter depends on us, then it has no substance, for if HaShem does not bring us up from here in His mercy — from our side it will be blocked and withheld, for the evil inclination is stronger than we are, as our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Sukkah 52b): 'A person's inclination is greater than he is' — as is derived in the Yalkut on Psalms (§563, on Psalm 35:10) regarding the verse 'who saves the poor from one stronger than he' — that is, from the evil inclination; see there."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:41:12.334409401Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}